div.div:
barbĭtŏs , m. (f. in the spurious epistle of Sappho, Ov. H. 15, 8;
I. v. infra ; found only in nom., acc., and voc.; plur. barbita, n., Aus. Ep. 44). = βάρβιτον ʽ-ος), a lyre, a lute (not before the Aug. per.): “age, dic Latinum, Barbite, carmen,” Hor. C. 1, 32, 4 ; 1, 1, 34; 3, 26, 4; Claud. Praef. ap. Nupt. Hon. et Mar. 10; Aus. Epigr. 44.—
II. Meton., the song played upon the lute: “non facit ad lacrimas barbitos ulla meas,” Ov. H. 15 . 8 (a spurious poem).
